          "text": "filename: docs/rename-photos.md\n```markdown\n# How to Rename a Folder of Holiday Photos with a Bash Loop\n\nThis guide shows how to automate the renaming of a folder of holiday photos using a bash loop. This is useful for organizing photos by date, making them easier to find, or preparing them for upload. The process involves iterating over files and applying a naming convention using shell scripting.\n\n## Example One: Date Prefix\nThis example adds a date prefix to each photo filename in the format YYYY-MM-DD. The script assumes the photos are in a folder named \"holiday_photos\".\n\n```bash\nfor file in holiday_photos/*; do\n    if [[ -f \"$file\" ]]; then\n        filename=$(basename \"$file\")\n        date_prefix=$(date -d \"2023-12-25\" +%Y-%m-%d)\n        new_name=\"${date_prefix}_${filename}\"\n        mv \"$file\" \"$new_name\"\n    fi\ndone\n```\n\n## Example Two: Lowercase Names\nThis example converts all file names to lowercase to ensure consistency and avoid case-sensitive issues in file systems.\n\n```bash\nfor file in holiday_photos/*; do\n    if [[ -f \"$file\" ]]; then\n        filename=$(basename \"$file\")\n        new_name=$(echo \"$filename\" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')\n        mv \"$file\" \"$new_name\"\n    fi\ndone\n```\n\n## Example Three: Number the Files\nThis example renames files sequentially with a number prefix, useful for creating a clean, ordered list of photos.\n\n```bash\ni=1\nfor file in holiday_photos/*; do\n    if [[ -f \"$file\" ]]; then\n        filename=$(basename \"$file\")\n        new_name=\"photo_${i}_${filename}\"\n        mv \"$file\" \"$new_name\"\n        i=$((i + 1))\n    fi\ndone\n```\n\nWrap Up: This bash script efficiently organizes holiday photos using simple loops and file operations. It improves searchability, maintains consistency, and reduces manual effort. You can adapt the naming rules to fit your needs. Always backup your files before running such scripts. The examples cover date-based, case-normalized, and numbered renaming. These techniques are reliable and widely applicable in photo management workflows.\n```",
